<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-14-nominals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Nominals</title>

  <objectives>
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Distinguish between nouns (form) and nominals (function/role in a sentence)</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify the various structures that can function as nominals</p></li>
      <li><p>Understand the five syntactic positions nominals occupy</p></li>
      <li><p>Analyze gerund phrases, infinitive phrases, and complement clauses as nominals</p></li>
      <li><p>Distinguish between that-clauses and wh-clauses as complement clauses</p></li>
      <li><p>Compare gerund, infinitive, and clausal nominal structures</p></li>
      <li><p>Key terms: Noun (form), Nominal (function), Gerund, Gerund phrase, Infinitive phrase (nominal), Complement clause, That-clause, Wh-clause, Extraposition, Nominalized adjective</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <p>Chapter 12 introduced adverbials—structures that modify verbs, adding information about time, place, manner, and reason. Chapter 13 introduced adjectivals—structures that modify nouns, describing or restricting what those nouns refer to. With this chapter, the triad is complete. <term>Nominals</term> are structures that fill the argument slots of a sentence: the subject, the direct object, the indirect object, the object of a preposition, and the subject complement. Together, these three functional categories—adverbial, adjectival, nominal—account for nearly every role a phrase or clause can play in English.</p>

  <p>What makes nominals surprising is the range of forms that can fill a position you might expect only a noun to occupy. Consider the subject slot: in <foreign>Happiness is rare</foreign>, a single noun fills it. But in <foreign>That she persisted surprised everyone</foreign>, an entire clause with its own subject and verb fills the same slot. In <foreign>Swimming every morning changed his life</foreign>, a phrase built on a verb form does the same. This chapter explains how noun phrases, pronouns, gerund phrases, infinitive phrases, and complement clauses can each step into the noun's shoes—and why recognizing them matters for both analysis and writing.</p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.1  NOUNS VS. NOMINALS                                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-141">
    <title>Nouns vs. Nominals: Form vs. Function</title>

    <p>You have already encountered the form-vs.-function distinction twice: in Chapter 12, where you saw that adverbs, prepositional phrases, noun phrases, and clauses can all serve the adverbial function; and in Chapter 13, where you saw that adjectives, nouns, participial phrases, and relative clauses can all serve the adjectival function. Here the same logic applies to the nominal function. The form of a word tells you its part of speech; the function tells you the role it plays in a sentence. A word or phrase that fills a noun-phrase slot—regardless of its internal structure—is functioning nominally.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Noun as Form</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A <term>noun</term> is a part of speech—a word class with particular morphological and syntactic characteristics (Chapter 5). Nouns can be pluralized, possess, follow determiners, and serve as subjects and objects. Examples: <foreign>book, happiness, Maria, government</foreign>.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Nominal as Function (Role in a Sentence)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A <term>nominal</term> is any word, phrase, or clause that functions in a position typically occupied by a noun phrase—as subject, object, or complement. The key insight: all nouns functioning as subjects or objects are nominals, but not all nominals are nouns.</p>

    <p>Consider a noun phrase filling the subject role:</p>

    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Role</cell><cell>Subj</cell><cell></cell><cell>Pred</cell><cell>DO</cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Phrase</cell><cell>NP</cell><cell></cell><cell>VP</cell><cell>NP</cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Word</cell><cell><foreign>The</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>students</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>read</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>books</foreign></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>POS</cell><cell>DET</cell><cell>N</cell><cell>V</cell><cell>N</cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <p>Now compare a gerund phrase filling the same subject role:</p>

    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Role</cell><cell>Subj</cell><cell></cell><cell>Pred</cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Phrase</cell><cell>GP</cell><cell></cell><cell>VP</cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Word</cell><cell><foreign>Reading</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>books</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>is</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>fun</foreign></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>POS</cell><cell>V(-ing)</cell><cell>N</cell><cell>V</cell><cell>ADJ</cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <p>Both <foreign>The students</foreign> and <foreign>Reading books</foreign> fill the subject slot. Their forms are completely different—a noun phrase and a gerund phrase—but their function is the same.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>The Five Nominal Positions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Unlike adverbials (which are optional) and adjectivals (which modify nouns), nominals fill the <em>argument</em> positions of a clause—the slots that the verb requires or that the sentence structure provides. Chapter 8 introduced these positions; here is a quick reference:</p>

    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>Position</cell>
        <cell>Location</cell>
        <cell>Example</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell>Subject</cell><cell>Before the verb</cell><cell><foreign><em>The dog</em> barked.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Direct Object</cell><cell>After a transitive verb</cell><cell><foreign>She read <em>the book</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Indirect Object</cell><cell>Before the direct object</cell><cell><foreign>She gave <em>me</em> a book.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Object of Preposition</cell><cell>After a preposition</cell><cell><foreign>We talked about <em>the problem</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Subject Complement</cell><cell>After a linking verb</cell><cell><foreign>She is <em>a doctor</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <p>See Section 14.3 for a fuller treatment of these positions with examples from all nominal types.</p>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.2  STRUCTURES THAT FUNCTION AS NOMINALS                    -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-142">
    <title>Structures That Function as Nominals</title>

    <p>The simplest nominal is what you would expect: a noun phrase with a determiner, perhaps some modifiers, and a noun at its head. But the nominal function is not restricted to noun phrases. Pronouns, complement clauses, gerund phrases, infinitive phrases, and even some adjectives can all occupy the same syntactic slots. The catalog below moves from the most typical to the more structurally complex, so you can see the full range of what English treats as equivalent to a noun phrase.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>1. Noun Phrases</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The prototypical nominal is a noun phrase:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>The book</em> is on the table.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I read <em>the interesting book</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>This is <em>my sister</em>.</foreign> (subject complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>2. Pronouns</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Pronouns replace entire noun phrases and fill all the same positions:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>She</em> arrived early.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I saw <em>them</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She gave <em>me</em> a book.</foreign> (indirect object)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>3. Gerund Phrases</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>The <foreign>-ing</foreign> form of a verb (gerund) heads phrases that function as nominals:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Swimming</em> is good exercise.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She enjoys <em>reading novels</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She succeeded by <em>working hard</em>.</foreign> (object of preposition)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>4. Infinitive Phrases</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Infinitive phrases (<foreign>to</foreign> + verb) can function as nominals:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To err</em> is human.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She wants <em>to succeed</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The goal is <em>to finish on time</em>.</foreign> (subject complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>5. That-clauses (Complement Clauses)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Clauses introduced by <foreign>that</foreign> function as nominals. These are also called <em>complement clauses</em> or <em>nominal clauses</em>:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That she resigned</em> surprised everyone.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>that she is honest</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The problem is <em>that we're out of time</em>.</foreign> (subject complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>6. Wh-clauses (Complement Clauses)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Clauses introduced by wh-words also function as nominals:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>What she said</em> was important.</foreign> (subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I don't know <em>where he went</em>.</foreign> (direct object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The question is <em>who will lead</em>.</foreign> (subject complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>7. Nominalized Adjectives</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Some adjectives, with a determiner, function nominally by referring to a group of people:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>The poor</em> need assistance.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>The accused</em> denied the charges.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.3  NOMINAL POSITION                                        -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-143">
    <title>Nominal Position</title>

    <p>One of the most useful things about the nominal function is that it gives you a systematic way to identify what role any noun-like structure is playing in a sentence. Rather than asking <q>Is this a noun?</q>—a question about form—you ask <q>What slot does it fill?</q>—a question about function. The answer is one of five positions: subject, direct object, indirect object, object of a preposition, or subject complement. Each position has a characteristic location in the sentence, and recognizing the position tells you immediately how the nominal relates to the verb and the rest of the clause.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Subject Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Before the main verb. The subject is what the sentence is about—the entity performing the action or being described:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>The students</em> studied.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That he won</em> surprised us.</foreign> (that-clause)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Swimming</em> is fun.</foreign> (gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To forgive</em> is divine.</foreign> (infinitive)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Direct Object Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>After a transitive verb. The direct object receives the action:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She read <em>the book</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I know <em>that she's coming</em>.</foreign> (that-clause)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He enjoys <em>playing chess</em>.</foreign> (gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She wants <em>to succeed</em>.</foreign> (infinitive)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Indirect Object Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Before the direct object. The indirect object is the recipient:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She gave <em>me</em> a book.</foreign> (pronoun)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>Tell <em>whoever asks</em> the truth.</foreign> (wh-clause)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Object of Preposition</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>After a preposition. The nominal completes the prepositional phrase:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>We talked about <em>the problem</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I'm interested in <em>what you said</em>.</foreign> (wh-clause)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She succeeded by <em>working hard</em>.</foreign> (gerund)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Subject Complement Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>After a linking verb. The complement renames or describes the subject:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She is <em>a doctor</em>.</foreign> (NP)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The problem is <em>that we're late</em>.</foreign> (that-clause)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>His hobby is <em>collecting coins</em>.</foreign> (gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The goal is <em>to finish on time</em>.</foreign> (infinitive)</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.4  NOUN PHRASE NOMINALS                                    -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-144">
    <title>Noun Phrase Nominals</title>

    <p>The noun phrase is the prototypical nominal—the structure that fills argument positions by default. Every other nominal type in this chapter can be understood as a non-NP structure stepping into a slot that an NP would normally occupy. Understanding the NP baseline makes the other types easier to grasp.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A noun phrase consists of a head noun with optional determiners, pre-modifiers, and post-modifiers (Chapter 7):</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><em>Simple:</em> <foreign><em>books</em></foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><em>With determiner:</em> <foreign><em>the book</em></foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><em>With modifiers:</em> <foreign><em>the interesting old book on the shelf</em></foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Role</cell><cell>Subj</cell><cell></cell><cell>Pred</cell><cell>DO</cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Phrase</cell><cell>NP</cell><cell></cell><cell>VP</cell><cell>NP</cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Word</cell><cell><foreign>The</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>student</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>read</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>books</foreign></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>POS</cell><cell>DET</cell><cell>N</cell><cell>V</cell><cell>N</cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>NPs can fill all five nominal positions (subject, DO, IO, object of preposition, complement).</p></li>
      <li><p>The head noun determines the number (singular/plural) and triggers verb agreement.</p></li>
      <li><p>Chapters 12 and 13 showed NPs functioning as adverbials and adjectivals—form is noun phrase, but function varies.</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.5  PRONOUN NOMINALS                                        -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-145">
    <title>Pronoun Nominals</title>

    <p>Pronouns are the most compact nominals—single words that replace entire noun phrases. Chapter 6 covered pronouns as a form class. Here the focus is on their nominal function: pronouns fill the same argument positions as full NPs, but they carry information about person, number, gender, and case in a single word.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Case and Position</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>English pronouns change form based on their nominal position (case):</p>

    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>Position</cell>
        <cell>Case</cell>
        <cell>Example</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell>Subject</cell><cell>Subjective (<foreign>I, she, he, they</foreign>)</cell><cell><foreign><em>She</em> arrived.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Direct/Indirect Object</cell><cell>Objective (<foreign>me, her, him, them</foreign>)</cell><cell><foreign>I saw <em>her</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Object of Preposition</cell><cell>Objective</cell><cell><foreign>He spoke to <em>them</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Subject Complement</cell><cell>Subjective (formal) or Objective (informal)</cell><cell><foreign>It is <em>I</em>.</foreign> / <foreign>It's <em>me</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell>Possessive</cell><cell>Possessive (<foreign>my/mine, her/hers</foreign>)</cell><cell><foreign><em>Her</em> book</foreign> / <foreign>The book is <em>hers</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>Pronouns are the only English nominals that change form based on their syntactic position (case marking).</p></li>
      <li><p>In formal usage, subject complements take subjective case: <foreign>It is I</foreign>. In informal speech, objective case is standard: <foreign>It's me</foreign>.</p></li>
      <li><p>See Chapter 6 for the full pronoun classification (personal, demonstrative, indefinite, relative, interrogative, reflexive).</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.6  GERUND PHRASE NOMINALS                                  -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-146">
    <title>Gerund Phrase Nominals</title>

    <p>A <term>gerund</term> is the <foreign>-ing</foreign> form of a verb functioning nominally. Gerunds present one of the clearest examples of the form-vs.-function distinction at work. Morphologically, <foreign>swimming</foreign> looks exactly like a present participle—the same <foreign>-ing</foreign> ending, the same verbal form. The difference is purely functional: when it modifies a noun (as in <foreign>the swimming dog</foreign>), it is a participle functioning adjectivally; when it fills a noun-phrase slot (as in <foreign>Swimming is good exercise</foreign>), it is a gerund functioning nominally. Because gerunds retain their verbal character, they can take their own objects and modifiers, which means a gerund phrase can be quite long even though it fills a single argument slot.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A <term>gerund phrase</term> consists of a gerund (the <foreign>-ing</foreign> form) plus any complements and modifiers:</p>

    <p>Gerund Phrase = (Possessive) + Gerund + (Complements/Modifiers)</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Swimming</em> is fun.</foreign> (simple gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Swimming laps</em> is good exercise.</foreign> (gerund + object)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>His swimming</em> improved.</foreign> (possessive + gerund)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Their constantly complaining</em> annoys everyone.</foreign> (possessive + modifier + gerund)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Gerund Functions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p><em>As subject:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Running marathons</em> requires dedication.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Finishing on time</em> seemed impossible.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As direct object:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She enjoys <em>cooking elaborate meals</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He avoided <em>making promises</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As object of preposition:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She succeeded by <em>working hard</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He's interested in <em>learning languages</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As subject complement:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>His favorite activity is <em>reading</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The challenge was <em>convincing the board</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Possessive with Gerunds</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Formal usage prefers possessive forms before gerunds:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I appreciated <em>his helping</em> us.</foreign> (formal)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I appreciated <em>him helping</em> us.</foreign> (informal)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>Both are acceptable in modern English, though the possessive is preferred in formal writing.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Role</cell><cell>Subj</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>Pred</cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Phrase</cell><cell>GP</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>VP</cell><cell>ADJP</cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Word</cell><cell><foreign>Running</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>every</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>morning</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>is</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>healthy</foreign></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>POS</cell><cell>V(-ing)</cell><cell>DET</cell><cell>N</cell><cell>V</cell><cell>ADJ</cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <p>The gerund phrase <foreign>Running every morning</foreign> fills the subject slot—a position normally occupied by a noun phrase.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>Gerunds look identical to present participles (<foreign>-ing</foreign>). The distinction is functional: gerunds are nominals, participles are adjectivals or part of verb phrases.</p></li>
      <li><p>Gerund phrases can fill all five nominal positions, including object of preposition (which infinitives cannot).</p></li>
      <li><p>In diagrams, label the gerund phrase as GP (gerund phrase) at the phrase level.</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.7  INFINITIVE PHRASE NOMINALS                              -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-147">
    <title>Infinitive Phrase Nominals</title>

    <p>Like gerund phrases, infinitive phrases are built on a verbal base, and they can take their own objects and modifiers. In Chapter 12 you saw infinitive phrases as adverbials of purpose (<foreign>She came to help</foreign>). In Chapter 13 you saw them as adjectivals modifying nouns (<foreign>a book to read</foreign>). Here the focus is on their nominal use—when an infinitive phrase fills a subject, object, or complement position. Unlike gerunds, infinitives have a narrower distribution as nominals: they appear frequently as subjects and complements, but only certain verbs allow an infinitive direct object.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Infinitive Phrase = <foreign>to</foreign> + Verb + (Complements/Modifiers)</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To swim</em> is fun.</foreign> (simple infinitive)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To finish on time</em> seemed impossible.</foreign> (infinitive + modifier)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To convince the board of our plan</em> took hours.</foreign> (infinitive + complex complement)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Infinitive Functions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p><em>As subject</em> (often with extraposition):</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>To err</em> is human.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>It</em> is difficult <em>to learn a new language</em>.</foreign> (extraposed)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As direct object:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She wants <em>to succeed</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>They decided <em>to leave early</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He hopes <em>to be promoted</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As subject complement:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>The goal is <em>to finish on time</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>Her dream is <em>to travel the world</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Extraposition</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>When infinitive subjects are long or complex, English often moves them to the end of the sentence and places the pronoun <foreign>it</foreign> at the front as a placeholder. This pattern is called <term>extraposition</term>:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>To explain all the details would take too long.</foreign> (full subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>It</em> would take too long <em>to explain all the details</em>.</foreign> (extraposed)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>To learn a new language at fifty is challenging.</foreign> (full subject)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>It</em> is challenging <em>to learn a new language at fifty</em>.</foreign> (extraposed)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>Extraposition is extremely common in both speech and writing. The extraposed version is often preferred because it places the main verb closer to the beginning of the sentence, making it easier to process.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Role</cell><cell>Subj</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>Pred</cell><cell>SC</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Phrase</cell><cell>VP</cell><cell></cell><cell>NP</cell><cell></cell><cell>VP</cell><cell>NP</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Word</cell><cell><foreign>To</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>win</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>the</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>race</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>was</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>her</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>only</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>goal</foreign></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>POS</cell><cell>PART</cell><cell>V</cell><cell>DET</cell><cell>N</cell><cell>V</cell><cell>PRON</cell><cell>ADJ</cell><cell>N</cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <p>The infinitive phrase <foreign>To win the race</foreign> fills the subject position.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>Nominal infinitives fill subject, direct object, and complement positions—but not object of preposition (use a gerund instead: <foreign>interested in learning</foreign>, not <foreign><delete>interested in to learn</delete></foreign>).</p></li>
      <li><p>Extraposition with <foreign>it</foreign> is the most common way to handle long infinitive subjects.</p></li>
      <li><p>The same infinitive form can be nominal (filling an argument slot), adverbial (expressing purpose), or adjectival (modifying a noun)—always check the position.</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.8  COMPLEMENT CLAUSES: THAT-CLAUSES                       -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-148">
    <title>Complement Clauses: That-Clauses</title>

    <p>A <term>complement clause</term> (also called a <em>nominal clause</em> or <em>noun clause</em>) is a full clause—with its own subject and predicate—that fills a nominal position in a larger sentence. The term <em>complement</em> here means that the clause completes the meaning of a verb, adjective, or noun. This section covers that-clauses; the next section covers wh-clauses. Together they are the two main types of complement clause in English.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A that-clause consists of the complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> followed by a complete clause (subject + predicate):</p>

    <p>That-clause = <foreign>that</foreign> + Subject + Predicate</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That she won</em> surprised no one.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>that she is honest</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Positions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p><em>As subject:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That she won</em> surprised no one.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That the earth is round</em> is well established.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As direct object</em> (<foreign>that</foreign> often omitted):</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>(that) she's honest</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He knows <em>(that) we're coming</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As subject complement:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>The truth is <em>that we need more time</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>My hope is <em>that things will improve</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>After adjectives:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I'm glad <em>that you came</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She was certain <em>that he was wrong</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Omitting <foreign>that</foreign></title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>When a that-clause is a direct object, the complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> can often be omitted with no change in meaning:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I think <em>(that)</em> she's right.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He said <em>(that)</em> he would come.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>When a that-clause is a subject, <foreign>that</foreign> cannot be omitted:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>That</em> she won surprised everyone.</foreign> (not <foreign><delete>She won surprised everyone.</delete></foreign>)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Role</cell><cell>Subj</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>Pred</cell><cell>DO</cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Phrase</cell><cell>NP</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>VP</cell><cell>NP</cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Word</cell><cell><foreign>I</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>believe</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>that</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>she</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>is</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>honest</foreign></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>POS</cell><cell>PRON</cell><cell>V</cell><cell>COMP</cell><cell>PRON</cell><cell>V</cell><cell>ADJ</cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <p>The that-clause <foreign>that she is honest</foreign> fills the direct object position after the verb <foreign>believe</foreign>.</p>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_001.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "know that you cry"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch13_that_clause_object.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "I know that you cry" showing a complement clause as direct object</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [PRON I]] [VP [V know] [CC [COMP that] [NP [PRON you]] [VP [V cry]]]]]</c></p>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_002.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "That you cry upsets me"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch13_that_clause_subject.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "That you cry upsets me" showing a complement clause as subject</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [CC [COMP That] [NP [PRON you]] [VP [V cry]]] [VP [V upsets] [NP [PRON me]]]]</c></p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>That-clauses report propositions—statements about the world that can be true or false.</p></li>
      <li><p>The complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> can be omitted in object position but not in subject position.</p></li>
      <li><p>Common verbs taking that-clause objects: <foreign>believe, know, think, say, hope, realize, admit, claim, doubt</foreign>.</p></li>
      <li><p>In diagrams, label the complementizer <foreign>that</foreign> as COMP.</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.9  COMPLEMENT CLAUSES: WH-CLAUSES                         -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-149">
    <title>Complement Clauses: Wh-Clauses</title>

    <p>The second major type of complement clause is the <term>wh-clause</term> (also called an <em>interrogative content clause</em> or <em>indirect question</em>). While that-clauses embed statements, wh-clauses embed questions. In <foreign>I know that she left</foreign>, the speaker states a fact; in <foreign>I know where she went</foreign>, the speaker reports the answer to an implicit question. The wh-word simultaneously introduces the clause and fills a grammatical role within it—much like a relative pronoun does in a relative clause (Chapter 13), but here the clause functions nominally rather than adjectivally.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Formation</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>A wh-clause consists of a wh-word followed by clause content. The wh-word both introduces the clause and fills a role within it:</p>

    <p>Wh-clause = Wh-word + (Subject) + Predicate</p>

    <tabular halign="left">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>Wh-word</cell>
        <cell>Asks about</cell>
        <cell>Example</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell><foreign>what</foreign></cell><cell>things/actions</cell><cell><foreign>I know <em>what happened</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>who/whom</foreign></cell><cell>people</cell><cell><foreign>She asked <em>who called</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>which</foreign></cell><cell>specific choice</cell><cell><foreign>Tell me <em>which one you prefer</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>where</foreign></cell><cell>place</cell><cell><foreign>I don't know <em>where he went</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>when</foreign></cell><cell>time</cell><cell><foreign>She asked <em>when we would arrive</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>why</foreign></cell><cell>reason</cell><cell><foreign>I wonder <em>why she left</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>how</foreign></cell><cell>manner/extent</cell><cell><foreign>Tell me <em>how you did it</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
      <row><cell><foreign>whether/if</foreign></cell><cell>yes/no</cell><cell><foreign>I wonder <em>whether she'll come</em>.</foreign></cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Positions</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p><em>As subject:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>What she discovered</em> changed everything.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Where they went</em> remains a mystery.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Whether he'll accept</em> is uncertain.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As direct object:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I don't know <em>what happened</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She asked <em>whether I was coming</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>Tell me <em>how you did it</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>As subject complement:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>The issue is <em>how we proceed</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>The question is <em>whether to accept</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>If vs. Whether</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Both <foreign>if</foreign> and <foreign>whether</foreign> introduce yes/no complement clauses, but they are not always interchangeable:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>Whether</foreign> can be a subject; <foreign>if</foreign> typically cannot:</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign><em>Whether</em> he comes remains to be seen.</foreign> ✓</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><delete>If he comes remains to be seen.</delete></foreign> ✗</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>Whether</foreign> allows <foreign>or not</foreign> immediately after; <foreign>if</foreign> does not:</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I don't know <em>whether or not</em> she's coming.</foreign> ✓</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign><delete>I don't know if or not she's coming.</delete></foreign> ✗</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>In general, <foreign>whether</foreign> is preferred in formal writing and required in subject position.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Labeling Table</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Role</cell><cell>Subj</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>Pred</cell><cell>DO</cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Phrase</cell><cell>NP</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>VP</cell><cell></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>Word</cell><cell><foreign>She</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>asked</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>whether</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>we</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>could</foreign></cell><cell><foreign>help</foreign></cell></row>
      <row bottom="minor"><cell>POS</cell><cell>PRON</cell><cell>V</cell><cell>COMP</cell><cell>PRON</cell><cell>MODAL</cell><cell>V</cell></row>
    </tabular>

    <image source="diagrams/new/ch14_table_003.png" width="65%">
      <description>Multi-level labeling table for "wonder if you cry"</description>
    </image>
    <image source="diagrams/new/ch13_if_clause.png" width="55%">
      <description>Syntax tree for "I wonder if you cry" showing an if-clause as direct object</description>
    </image>
    <p><c>[S [NP [PRON I]] [VP [V wonder] [CC [COMP if] [NP [PRON you]] [VP [V cry]]]]]</c></p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Key Points</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <ul>
      <li><p>Wh-clauses embed questions as nominals. The word order within the clause is declarative, not interrogative: <foreign>I know where she went</foreign> (not <foreign><delete>I know where did she go</delete></foreign>).</p></li>
      <li><p>Use <foreign>whether</foreign> (not <foreign>if</foreign>) in subject position and before <foreign>or not</foreign>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Common verbs taking wh-clause objects: <foreign>know, wonder, ask, tell, explain, decide, understand</foreign>.</p></li>
    </ul>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 14.10  COMPARING NOMINAL STRUCTURES                           -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="ch-14-nominals-sec-1410">
    <title>Comparing Nominal Structures</title>

    <p>Different nominal structures may seem interchangeable but often carry subtle differences in meaning or style. If you can say <foreign>I like swimming</foreign> and also <foreign>I like to swim</foreign>, you might wonder whether the choice of gerund versus infinitive ever matters. The answer is: sometimes no, sometimes yes, and sometimes the choice changes the meaning of the sentence entirely. This section gives you the tools to choose among nominal structures deliberately rather than by habit.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Gerund vs. Infinitive</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Many verbs take either gerund or infinitive objects with little difference:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I like <em>swimming</em>.</foreign> / <foreign>I like <em>to swim</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>She started <em>working</em>.</foreign> / <foreign>She started <em>to work</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>But some verbs show clear differences:</p>

    <p><em>Gerund only:</em> <foreign>enjoy, avoid, finish, deny, consider, keep, mind</foreign></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She enjoys <em>swimming</em>.</foreign> (not <foreign><delete>to swim</delete></foreign>)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Infinitive only:</em> <foreign>want, hope, decide, agree, refuse, plan, promise</foreign></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>She wants <em>to swim</em>.</foreign> (not <foreign><delete>swimming</delete></foreign>)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Different meanings with gerund vs. infinitive:</em></p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I stopped <em>smoking</em>.</foreign> (quit the habit)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I stopped <em>to smoke</em>.</foreign> (paused in order to smoke)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I remember <em>locking</em> the door.</foreign> (I recall the past act of locking)</p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I remember <em>to lock</em> the door.</foreign> (I don't forget to do it)</p></li>
    </ul>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>That-clause vs. Infinitive</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Some verbs allow both:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>that she's honest</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>I believe <em>her to be honest</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>The infinitive construction may sound more formal.</p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>That-clause vs. Gerund</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>Some verbs take either:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><foreign>He admitted <em>that he was wrong</em>.</foreign></p></li>
      <li><p><foreign>He admitted <em>being wrong</em>.</foreign></p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>The gerund is often more concise.</p>

  </section>

