      <p><em>Exercise 24.</em> Consider the verb <foreign>remember</foreign> in these two sentences:</p>

      <p>a) <foreign>I remember locking the door.</foreign></p>

      <p>b) <foreign>I remember to lock the door.</foreign></p>

      <p>Explain the meaning difference:</p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Transformation and Style</title>
      </paragraphs>

      <p><em>Exercise 25.</em> Transform the following statement into four different nominal structures:</p>

      <p>Statement: <foreign>The experiment succeeded.</foreign></p>

      <p>a) As a that-clause functioning as subject:</p>

      <p>b) As a gerund phrase functioning as subject:</p>

      <p>c) As a wh-clause (embedded question) functioning as direct object (use a verb like <foreign>wonder</foreign> or <foreign>ask</foreign>):</p>

      <p>d) As an infinitive phrase after the verb <foreign>seem</foreign>:</p>

      <p><em>Exercise 26.</em> In formal writing, sentences like <q><foreign>That she resigned surprised everyone</foreign></q> are sometimes revised to <q><foreign>It surprised everyone that she resigned</foreign></q> (extraposition).</p>

      <p>a) What is extraposition?</p>

      <p>b) When might a writer prefer the extraposed version?</p>

      <p>c) When might a writer prefer the non-extraposed version (with the clause in subject position)?</p>
    </subsection>

